{
  "id": "COMPOUND:18885 - PROTEIN:5864",
  "verbalization": "Dihydrostreptomycin Sulfate appears to exert its biological effects through a shared target of Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, which is subsequently targeted by multiple compounds, including Yuanhunine, Rhetsinine, and 5-Hydroxy-2-Methylchromone-7-O-Rutinoside. These compounds all converge on Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, suggesting a potential mechanism of action involving the modulation of this mitochondrial protein. The presence of multiple targets at this level implies that Dihydrostreptomycin Sulfate may act through a common pathway, possibly by inhibiting Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, and thereby influencing cellular processes in U373 MG cells. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Thioredoxin, mitochondrial in the context of drug action, and underscores the potential for Dihydrostreptomycin Sulfate to exert its effects through this critical molecular node. The evidence supports a mechanism where Dihydrostreptomycin Sulfate may act as a modulator of Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, which in turn affects U373 MG cells, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
